## Canonical References

Which extension holds the reference implementation of a pattern. The reviewer and
the builders consult this before accepting that code "matches com_X" — an older
extension carrying a rejected pattern is how one defect becomes many.

Copy this file to `includes/joomla-canonical-references.md` and fill it in for your
own extensions. That path is gitignored: the entries name real projects and
sometimes real paths, which belong to your machine rather than to this repository.

An entry earns its place when an agent would otherwise have to guess. If two
extensions implement a pattern and one is better, say which. If the written
standard in `includes/` already settles it, no entry is needed.

Keep each reference specific enough to open — the extension, its version, and the
files that matter.

### Component and List View

| Pattern | Canonical reference |
|---|---|
| `getListQuery()`, filters, shared list-query traits | `com_example` |
| Trash / Empty Trash list-view toolbar | `com_example` |
| List sorting from column headings only | `com_example` 1.0.0, `forms/filter_items.xml` and `tmpl/items/default.php` |

### Editing and Locking

| Pattern | Canonical reference |
|---|---|
| Checkout / check-in: Table null support, lock icon, Check-in button, edit-view guard | `com_example` 1.0.0 — for this pattern only |

### Data Access and Services

| Pattern | Canonical reference |
|---|---|
| Service to DataModel to Table pipeline | `com_example` |
| Single point of authorisation | `com_example`, `Administrator\Service\AuthorisationService` |

### Anything Else

`includes/` — the written rule outranks any file. Where no entry exists here, the
standards are the reference, not whichever extension happens to be open.
